# LED Filament Hexpansion for Tildagon Badge

A very simple hexpansion that takes the 3.3V from the Tildagon badge and adds a resistor and a pot for current-controlled dimming. This allows you to control the brightness of your LED and save battery life on the badge. 


![](Filament_Hexpansions.jpg)

## Usage

There are two large and ornate pads that can accomodate a wide variety of LEDs and LED filaments on both sides of the badge. This means that regardless of the polarity of your filament, you can add it to the front or back and have it be the right way around.



## Components

The pot is a BOURNS 3362P-1-101LF 100 ohm pot, and the resistor is really any old choose-your-wattage 10 ohm resistor. These can of course be varied in value, but this seems to provide a good variety of brightnesses.  


## Construction
1. Solder the 10 ohm resistor
2. Solder the 100 ohm potentiometer
3. Solder your choice of LED/filament in place

Notes:
* For edge-lit acrylic signs, I use a 19mm "Edison bulb" filament. These can be somewhat tricky to solder in place. Here's how I do it myself:
	- Typically, these have a hole in the *positive* lead, so the positive lead is at the bottom of the picture below. Yes it looks like a minus sign, and no I do not know why they do this. Make sure you have the postive lead on the positive pad ![](filament1.jpg)
	- For *some* filaments, they are brighter on one side than the other. It's best to power them up (any 3v source is fine) and check. I've found that it's *usually* it's the side with thinner silicone, so in this image, it's the right side. However, any orientation will still work, it just may not be quite as bright as it could be! ![](filament2.jpg)
	- Place the filament on the upper two leads of the potentiometer in the correct orientation (lead with hole on positive pad, thinner silicone 'up'), ![](board1.jpg)
	- Use reverse tweezers (or possibly an alligator clip or foreceps - gently) to hold the filament in place and in the correct orientation. I use reverse tweezers, which hold it in place firmly enough to then be able to bend the leads down gently to touch the pads.![](board2.jpg)
	- Dab a bit of flux on the pad and lead (not necessary, but it *really* does help), and solder each lead in place. If you use too much solder, it will try to fill the hole, making it difficult to add the screws later. If this happens, use a solder sucker to remove some of the solder. Filaments seem quite heat tolerant: I've not yet seen one die of overheating. However, they *are* somewhat fragile. You can snap them in half or snap an end off with too much force, so use a little caution.![](board3.jpg)  
	- If you really struggle to solder this type of filament in place, you could just choose to not solder it and hold it in place with the screws/nuts through the holes. You will need to rotate the filament so that the leads are parallel to the board, and possibly bend them (*gently*) upwards. This does work, but I don't know what the chances are of the filament falling out.
	- If you're using one of my bits of acrylic, they go on the back of the board immediately above the filament. They are cut in reverse, so the engraved side is the back.
	
